00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:12.540
As we see many of our customers
adopting APIs and using that

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:16.710
as a way to transact digitally
with many of the businesses--

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:18.510
many of the partners
and employees

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:20.730
and external entities--

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:24.430
we're going to walk you through
the role of the API team.

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:26.040
This is a team who
is traditionally

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:30.390
responsible for working,
standing up APIs, securing

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:31.650
them, so on and so forth.

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:34.250
So today is going to be a
very interactive session.

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:37.400
We're going to duel back and
forth between slides and demos

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.650
to give you a taste of how
that experience looks like.

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:45.250
Why APIs?

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:48.640
When you hear about things
such as digital transformation

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:53.380
it essentially means many
modern enterprises today

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:57.100
are transacting their
business through APIs.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:01.540
So all the core capabilities
that have been locked in some

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:04.760
systems or records--
such as ERP systems--

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.070
some of the systems
which are home grown

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:11.660
are now unlocked as APIs
in this modern world.

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:13.780
Which means you
can take those APIs

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:16.940
and not only create some
excellent productivity

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:20.810
applications internally, you can
take them out to your partners

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:22.940
and build this whole ecosystem.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:24.679
In the morning in the keynote--

00:02:24.679 --> 00:02:26.720
if some of you guys got
a chance to attend that--

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.290
the different kinds of
ecosystems, partners,

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:32.730
employees, and then
even the outside world

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:36.410
to the industry and
then external entities.

00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:41.390
APIs are becoming more and more
central to every organization

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:44.810
that is trying to participate
in the digital world.

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:48.950
We take a slightly
opinionated view about APIs,

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:52.280
and we start with what we call
as the digital value chain.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:55.670
It's actually a very simple
concept if you think about it.

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:57.890
At the end of the
day, all what matters

00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:01.820
is the entity to your
left or my right,

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:05.720
which is the customer
or the end user.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:09.810
It's all about delivering
delightful and connected

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:17.370
experiences to those end
users through an app.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:18.590
An app can be of any kind.

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:19.490
It could be a mobile app.

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:20.864
It could be an
app which is built

00:03:20.864 --> 00:03:24.180
in the web or even a setup box.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:28.820
Well, there are developers
who build those apps, right?

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:34.090
And they build those apps
using well curated APIs which

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:37.030
are managed by an API team.

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:41.680
And this API team
comprises of API developers

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:44.260
who will use a platform
such as Apigee Edge

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:48.550
to manage, secure,
publish to the APIs.

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:51.120
And essentially the
API layer really sits

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:54.270
on top of any back-end
system that you may have.

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:57.820
It could be something which
is already service enabled.

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:00.990
It could be an old system
which has some SOAP-based API,

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:02.100
perhaps.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:03.740
Or perhaps there's
some legacy systems

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:08.580
which are unlocked using some
kind of a middleware solution.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:12.120
So we're going to examine
what the data value chain is

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:18.160
and how you can essentially
use these core constructs to do

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:19.570
something very simple.

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:22.930
It's all about
ensuring that you have

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:25.660
massive adoption of these APIs.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:27.970
As you start in
a digital journey

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:30.460
and start to transact more
and more through APIs,

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:32.290
adoption is key.

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:36.010
It's only as good as
people using your APIs

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:39.970
that they are going to start
giving you the benefits

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:41.470
that they promise.

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:42.880
It's also about scale.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:45.730
You need to be able to
rely on a platform that

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:47.620
can support massive
scale, and we're

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.750
talking about internal scales.

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:53.260
As a platform, Apigee Edge
supports billions of API calls

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:54.580
in a day in the Cloud.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:56.440
And we have several
of our customers

00:04:56.440 --> 00:04:59.860
who are actually supporting
the exact same capacity

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:01.920
in their own data centers today.

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:03.550
It's also about control.

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:05.650
Ensuring that people have
this awesome experience

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:07.030
comes at a cost.

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:09.250
You want to ensure
that if APIs are

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:11.470
the way you're
transacting your business,

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:14.050
it's done in a
careful, curated way.

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:16.570
You have a lot of
security around ensuring

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:18.730
that only the people,
or the entities,

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:24.340
or the apps, who should get
access to those APIs do so.

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:28.570
And in the end, all the tools
and the platform capabilities

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:32.350
really put a focus on
trying to give you all that

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:34.675
at a very fast pace or agility.

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:40.430
I'm going to turn it over
to Greg for this piece.

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:42.940
He's going to walk us
through what the Apigee Edge

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:44.330
platform is all about.

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:47.600
And then both him and me are
going to duel back and forth

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:51.320
in showing you through a live
demo of the journey of an API

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:54.330
developer and how they use
a platform to build APIs.

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:54.830
Greg?

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:55.746
GREG BRAIL: Excellent.

00:05:55.746 --> 00:05:56.510
Hey, I'm Greg.

00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:59.270
I'm going to be talking a little
bit about the Apigee platform

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:01.490
and a little bit about
what we actually do here.

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:04.130
So people always ask,
what does Apigee do?

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:05.890
If we looked at that
digital value chain,

00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:08.360
there were really two
things that were happening.

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:09.980
We were connecting
the developers--

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:12.390
the people who build
apps that use APIs--

00:06:12.390 --> 00:06:13.680
with the API team.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:16.640
The people who actually
create the APIs that they use.

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:18.350
And for that we
have to have things

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:21.560
like developer
portals, documentation,

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:24.710
self-service onboarding
tools, so that a developer can

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:26.900
click on a button and get
the security credentials

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:29.360
they need to make
API calls right away.

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:31.160
There's also a
technical connection

00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:35.330
where we're doing things like
adding security to API calls,

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:38.670
doing rate limiting,
caching, transforming APIs

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:40.820
from one format to another.

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:43.580
The Apigee Edge product is
responsible for doing really

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:45.110
both of those things.

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:48.050
And if we look at the
components of the product,

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:49.790
there are a couple
of different pieces.

00:06:49.790 --> 00:06:51.890
Down at the bottom is
what I'm talking about.

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:54.980
Between the application
and the API itself

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:57.170
we have a set of
runtime services,

00:06:57.170 --> 00:06:59.090
and that's where we
can insert policies.

00:06:59.090 --> 00:07:00.465
And Prithpal is
going to show you

00:07:00.465 --> 00:07:02.600
how we create those policies,
and what they can do.

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:04.820
And those policies can
do all sorts of things,

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:06.740
like I said, security,
rate limiting.

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:08.245
You'll get the
whole story there.

00:07:08.245 --> 00:07:09.620
And then on top
there are a bunch

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:11.180
of services that enable that.

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:13.310
So for instance, there's
a management component

00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:16.400
that is responsible and
supplies both a user interface

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:19.760
and an API for manipulating
all of these components.

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:23.210
Programming the proxies,
configuring the developers

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:24.740
and the applications,
and everything

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:26.960
that Prithpal is
going to do in the UI

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:29.489
has an associated API as well.

00:07:29.489 --> 00:07:31.280
There are also a set
of developer services.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:34.100
Those are the developer
portals, and the tools

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:36.440
that we can use in
order to construct a web

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:40.610
presence for our API, so a
developer can really connect

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:42.472
and learn how to use the API.

00:07:42.472 --> 00:07:44.180
And there are a set
of analytics services

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:47.372
that are gathering metadata
about all of the API traffic

00:07:47.372 --> 00:07:48.830
and presenting it
in different ways

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:51.770
so that you can report on
it, create custom reports,

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:53.540
and see all sorts of
information about how

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:55.540
the API is being used.

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:58.490
Now, Apigee Edge is a
product for all this.

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:02.750
Companies such as Google
have API management systems

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:05.240
that they built themselves
for their own APIs.

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:07.370
What Apigee Edge
really is is a product

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:10.820
that allows anybody to
create the API management

00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:13.460
system that they
need for their API

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:16.040
and that fits in
with the requirements

00:08:16.040 --> 00:08:18.080
of their enterprise.

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:21.780
Now, I don't know if any of you
are Apigee customers already,

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:23.780
but I wanted to talk a
little bit about actually

00:08:23.780 --> 00:08:25.610
how the product is deployed.

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:27.890
The entire product we
described there actually

00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:30.750
can run in a couple of
different form factors.

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:32.216
We deliver it as a service.

00:08:32.216 --> 00:08:34.340
It's available as a service,
which means everything

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:36.020
you see in the demo today--

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:39.080
it's all multitenant,
self-service Cloud App just

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:42.260
as you come to expect from
the Google Cloud Platform--

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:45.020
where Apigee is actually
sitting in between API

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:48.530
calls being made from
the client to the server,

00:08:48.530 --> 00:08:51.450
and it's actually the runtime
is going through our Cloud.

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:53.450
The entire product
is also available

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:55.220
for on-premises deployment.

00:08:55.220 --> 00:08:57.140
So we have a number of
customers-- typically

00:08:57.140 --> 00:08:59.390
large customers-- who
have lots of data centers.

00:08:59.390 --> 00:09:02.242
Because contrary to what
we might say sometimes,

00:09:02.242 --> 00:09:04.700
there's still a lot of data
centers out there in the world.

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:08.660
And customers often want to have
their API, and all of their API

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:10.580
traffic, and all of
the management of it

00:09:10.580 --> 00:09:13.020
controlled by their own software
running in their own data

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:13.700
center.

00:09:13.700 --> 00:09:15.380
And finally there's
a hybrid model.

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:17.180
And I'm going to get
to that a little bit,

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:19.280
because that's some very
interesting stuff that

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:21.620
is starting to emerge
that is starting

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:24.740
to meet our customers where
they really want to be.

00:09:24.740 --> 00:09:27.470
If you look at the traditional
way to use Apigee--

00:09:27.470 --> 00:09:30.470
sort of a big, big product
that sits in the middle.

00:09:30.470 --> 00:09:33.020
So if we look on the top, you
may have mobile apps and web

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:36.950
apps that are making API calls
from the internet to back-ends

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:40.310
that are down on the bottom
within an enterprise network.

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:44.030
And in using the Apigee
Edge as a service, typically

00:09:44.030 --> 00:09:45.740
Apigee is running
in the internet.

00:09:45.740 --> 00:09:49.106
The API traffic is going to
Apigee somewhere in the Cloud,

00:09:49.106 --> 00:09:51.230
and it is actually connecting
back to the back-end.

00:09:51.230 --> 00:09:53.420
And interestingly enough,
the runtime component

00:09:53.420 --> 00:09:55.910
of Apigee Edge can run
in the Google Cloud.

00:09:55.910 --> 00:09:57.890
It can also run on
the Amazon Cloud.

00:09:57.890 --> 00:09:59.990
Because the truth is we
have a lot of customers

00:09:59.990 --> 00:10:03.140
out there whose API back-ends
are already in Amazon.

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:04.580
And we're committed
to making sure

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:08.160
that that runtime is available
in both of those locations,

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:11.642
so that people can choose and
they can put their API proxies

00:10:11.642 --> 00:10:13.100
where they actually
need them to be

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:15.770
based on the geography
of their actual network.

00:10:15.770 --> 00:10:17.500
But we also offer
a hybrid option.

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:20.690
And that hybrid option allows
you to have the API gateway--

00:10:20.690 --> 00:10:22.610
the thing that proxies
the traffic located

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:23.960
wherever you want--

00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:26.100
without having the rest
of Apigee come with it.

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:30.050
So things like analytics and
management of the API stuff

00:10:30.050 --> 00:10:33.110
come as a service,
whereas the gateway itself

00:10:33.110 --> 00:10:37.400
becomes something that you can
deploy on your own network.

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:39.320
One of the things
we're doing this week

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:42.890
is we're actually introducing a
new option for the Apigee Edge

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:45.632
product called Edge Hybrid.

00:10:45.632 --> 00:10:47.840
And we're actually announcing
an early access program

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:50.810
that we're signing up selected
customers for this week.

00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:52.280
And what that's
allowing us to do

00:10:52.280 --> 00:10:53.960
is allowing us to mix
and match and have

00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:56.030
sort of the best of
both of these worlds.

00:10:56.030 --> 00:10:59.660
Where the runtime components,
the analytics components,

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:03.860
and the control components stay
as a service within Apigee,

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:05.990
but the gateway itself,
the proxies, the traffic

00:11:05.990 --> 00:11:07.129
can go anywhere.

00:11:07.129 --> 00:11:09.170
And we're introducing a
new open source component

00:11:09.170 --> 00:11:10.970
that we're calling APID.

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:12.740
And one of the
things that APID does

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:16.490
is it sort of acts as an
API for API management.

00:11:16.490 --> 00:11:21.140
So it offers services for
things like verifying API keys,

00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:23.060
computing distributed
quota values,

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:25.280
and gathering API analytics.

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:27.290
And then we can have
a variety of gateways

00:11:27.290 --> 00:11:29.270
that actually run in
your own data center,

00:11:29.270 --> 00:11:31.220
or inside a
Kubernetes container,

00:11:31.220 --> 00:11:33.650
or inside Pivotal Cloud
Foundry, or wherever

00:11:33.650 --> 00:11:35.840
you need them to be
that can then use

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:37.622
APID to supply those services.

00:11:37.622 --> 00:11:39.080
And in the end,
we're giving people

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:41.424
a very flexible
architecture where

00:11:41.424 --> 00:11:43.340
we're going to give them
very good performance

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:44.420
and a lot of robustness.

00:11:44.420 --> 00:11:46.669
Because the truth is that
there are a lot of different

00:11:46.669 --> 00:11:48.980
places where APIs need to run.

00:11:48.980 --> 00:11:51.184
API don't just run
in big data centers.

00:11:51.184 --> 00:11:52.850
They don't just run
on the Google Cloud.

00:11:52.850 --> 00:11:56.640
Increasingly, APIs run
on the backs of stores.

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:58.354
APIs run in airports.

00:11:58.354 --> 00:12:00.270
There are all kinds of
places where these APIs

00:12:00.270 --> 00:12:02.090
back-ends live,
and we want to be

00:12:02.090 --> 00:12:04.820
able to put a component of
the API management system

00:12:04.820 --> 00:12:07.622
in all those places.

00:12:07.622 --> 00:12:09.330
Now I want to get a
little bit to talking

00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:12.390
about the lifecycle on the
demo we're going to talk about.

00:12:12.390 --> 00:12:15.090
We think really that all APIs
go through a very similar

00:12:15.090 --> 00:12:16.860
lifecycle like this.

00:12:16.860 --> 00:12:19.444
We're going to start at
the top with API design.

00:12:19.444 --> 00:12:21.360
But we're going to move
on to how you actually

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:23.550
design your API and
how you share that API

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:24.870
design with others.

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:27.090
How you actually turn
that API into something

00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:28.590
that's on the internet.

00:12:28.590 --> 00:12:31.020
How you add layers of
security to your API using

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:32.610
industry standards.

00:12:32.610 --> 00:12:35.430
How you publish your API to
create a developer portal,

00:12:35.430 --> 00:12:37.140
and a web presence
developers can

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:39.330
use to sign up for your API.

00:12:39.330 --> 00:12:41.370
What to do if your API
gets a lot of traffic.

00:12:41.370 --> 00:12:42.930
How is it going to scale?

00:12:42.930 --> 00:12:44.790
How are we going to
monitor what's going on

00:12:44.790 --> 00:12:47.010
and get analytics, so
we can understand how

00:12:47.010 --> 00:12:49.200
the API is going to be used?

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:52.294
And there are lots and lots
of ways to monetize APIs.

00:12:52.294 --> 00:12:53.710
And one of the
ways is, of course,

00:12:53.710 --> 00:12:55.392
to charge for API calls.

00:12:55.392 --> 00:12:57.600
And if that's an option that
works for your business,

00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:01.130
that's something we're
going to talk about as well.

00:13:01.130 --> 00:13:02.630
We're going to start
that, and we're

00:13:02.630 --> 00:13:04.896
going to trade off a
little bit on the demo

00:13:04.896 --> 00:13:06.770
and switch from one to
another, but we're not

00:13:06.770 --> 00:13:08.120
going to switch just yet.

00:13:08.120 --> 00:13:10.010
I haven't said the magic word.

00:13:10.010 --> 00:13:11.630
So let's start with design.

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:14.644
And Prithpal is going to be
the person who does this,

00:13:14.644 --> 00:13:16.310
and I'm going to ask
him some questions.

00:13:16.310 --> 00:13:18.620
I'm an API developer.

00:13:18.620 --> 00:13:20.306
I've heard of swagger.

00:13:20.306 --> 00:13:21.680
And I hear about
something called

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:24.740
Open API, which sounds
like a less exciting way

00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:26.150
to say swagger.

00:13:26.150 --> 00:13:28.910
But as I understand
it, I can design

00:13:28.910 --> 00:13:31.957
and I can describe
my API in Open API.

00:13:31.957 --> 00:13:34.040
And if I do that, there's
all kinds of neat things

00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:36.140
I can do to generate
cool documentation

00:13:36.140 --> 00:13:38.000
and to share my
API documentation

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:39.290
with other people.

00:13:39.290 --> 00:13:42.120
Can you show us a little
bit how we can do that?

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:43.639
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: Absolutely.

00:13:43.639 --> 00:13:44.180
You're right.

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:47.720
Open API is a great
way to be able to share

00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:48.770
what the API does.

00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:50.540
It's an excellent
contract which can

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:53.510
be used to not only create
API but also share that

00:13:53.510 --> 00:13:56.210
across different developers,
so they have a common sense

00:13:56.210 --> 00:13:58.780
of what an API looks like.

00:13:58.780 --> 00:14:00.740
We're on the demo
screen out here.

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:04.580
What you see out here is
