00:16:11.290 --> 00:16:14.800
start examining
this simple API doc,

00:16:14.800 --> 00:16:18.220
it very clearly describes
what this API should be doing.

00:16:18.220 --> 00:16:20.950
In this case, we have
certain resources

00:16:20.950 --> 00:16:23.170
which have been declared
out here, such as how

00:16:23.170 --> 00:16:25.750
do I query a specific hotel?

00:16:25.750 --> 00:16:28.510
So you have different
kinds of options

00:16:28.510 --> 00:16:30.040
provided to you out there.

00:16:30.040 --> 00:16:31.807
Options to update hotels.

00:16:31.807 --> 00:16:34.140
In this case, we're going to
use the backdrop of a hotel

00:16:34.140 --> 00:16:35.230
as an example.

00:16:35.230 --> 00:16:38.080
And then you also have
the ability for you

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:41.110
to actually get a list
of all the hotels.

00:16:41.110 --> 00:16:44.170
So, Greg, as we just
were discussing,

00:16:44.170 --> 00:16:46.150
the Open API spec
gives you the ability

00:16:46.150 --> 00:16:47.890
to communicate your intent.

00:16:47.890 --> 00:16:51.630
And in this case,
the Open API editor--

00:16:51.630 --> 00:16:54.190
or Open API, it used to
be only known as swagger--

00:16:54.190 --> 00:16:56.320
is integrated right
into the product.

00:16:56.320 --> 00:16:58.390
Once you have
imported the spec, you

00:16:58.390 --> 00:17:00.880
can actually collaborate
with other API developers

00:17:00.880 --> 00:17:03.730
in the team, so they can
edit, make some mods,

00:17:03.730 --> 00:17:05.540
perhaps add some changes.

00:17:05.540 --> 00:17:08.829
And then you can take it to the
next step of that publishing

00:17:08.829 --> 00:17:10.875
or to actually creating an API.

00:17:10.875 --> 00:17:11.500
GREG BRAIL: OK.

00:17:11.500 --> 00:17:12.380
Cool.

00:17:12.380 --> 00:17:15.160
And I should point out that
Open API is an open standard.

00:17:15.160 --> 00:17:17.790
It's controlled by the
Open API foundation.

00:17:17.790 --> 00:17:20.020
The Version 2.0 is the
version of the spec

00:17:20.020 --> 00:17:21.220
that we're looking at here.

00:17:21.220 --> 00:17:23.308
There's a version 3.0
that's in the works

00:17:23.308 --> 00:17:25.599
and about to be published
that takes into consideration

00:17:25.599 --> 00:17:27.490
a lot of the inputs
people have had.

00:17:27.490 --> 00:17:30.509
But we've been working with Open
API for a couple of years now.

00:17:30.509 --> 00:17:32.800
And we've found that it's
become the de facto standard,

00:17:32.800 --> 00:17:34.660
and essentially the
most popular format

00:17:34.660 --> 00:17:37.434
out there for a free
API specification.

00:17:37.434 --> 00:17:39.350
There are some cool
things you can do with it.

00:17:39.350 --> 00:17:42.580
You can even generate as
SDKs for various languages

00:17:42.580 --> 00:17:45.610
from the Open API spec, which
is sometimes something that's

00:17:45.610 --> 00:17:48.730
very handy to be able to do.

00:17:48.730 --> 00:17:51.160
Let's go back to the
slides for a sec.

00:17:51.160 --> 00:17:54.310
And so we talked a little bit
about the spec and what open is

00:17:54.310 --> 00:17:55.480
and how it is.

00:17:55.480 --> 00:17:57.310
So once I have a spec,
now what do I do?

