00:22:47.350 --> 00:22:50.715
In this case, I will use
something as simple as Spike

00:22:50.715 --> 00:22:53.470
Arrest policy.

00:22:53.470 --> 00:22:54.990
The Spike Arrest policy--

00:22:54.990 --> 00:22:59.790
think of this as a system-wide
policy which can make sure

00:22:59.790 --> 00:23:04.159
that only a specific amount
of transactions per second,

00:23:04.159 --> 00:23:06.450
or transactional per minute,
whatever that rate happens

00:23:06.450 --> 00:23:09.750
to be, it can control
that so that none

00:23:09.750 --> 00:23:13.050
of your back-end
systems crash and burn.

00:23:13.050 --> 00:23:15.690
As we know, that back-end
API is running somewhere.

00:23:15.690 --> 00:23:18.540
It's either in the Cloud,
or it's running on-premises,

00:23:18.540 --> 00:23:21.150
and it has some finite capacity.

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:24.570
So in this case, I can make
those quick changes using

00:23:24.570 --> 00:23:27.330
some configuration options.

00:23:27.330 --> 00:23:29.910
In this case, for the
purpose of the demo,

00:23:29.910 --> 00:23:32.370
I'm just changing it
to three per minute.

00:23:32.370 --> 00:23:37.110
What this means is do not
allow any number of API calls,

00:23:37.110 --> 00:23:40.590
or only allow three
calls per minute.

00:23:40.590 --> 00:23:45.180
Just a very simple policy
that I've added out here.

00:23:45.180 --> 00:23:47.070
And at this point in
time, I would also

00:23:47.070 --> 00:23:50.720
like to call out the trace tool.

00:23:50.720 --> 00:23:53.900
For those who attended
the keynote in the morning

00:23:53.900 --> 00:23:55.880
may have seen the trace tool.

00:23:55.880 --> 00:23:58.260
It was flashed briefly.

00:23:58.260 --> 00:24:01.560
The trace tool gives a
very powerful mechanism

00:24:01.560 --> 00:24:03.630
for the API developer.

00:24:03.630 --> 00:24:06.240
As I'm building
out API proxies, it

00:24:06.240 --> 00:24:10.000
gives me some vital checkpoints
and information to make sure--

00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:12.940
are the policies which you've
added in working as expected?

00:24:12.940 --> 00:24:16.270
So think about this as
a web-based testing tool

00:24:16.270 --> 00:24:19.110
throughout my API
development process.

00:24:19.110 --> 00:24:21.870
So I'll just leave
the trace session on.

00:24:21.870 --> 00:24:29.510
I'll go back to my client,
and I will call this API.

00:24:29.510 --> 00:24:33.420
And when I do this, you can see
that there was a trace which

00:24:33.420 --> 00:24:34.950
was recorded here.

00:24:34.950 --> 00:24:37.290
I can hover over
some of those things

00:24:37.290 --> 00:24:39.510
and gather some
wider information.

00:24:39.510 --> 00:24:41.340
In this case, it
tells me exactly what

00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:43.770
was received from the user.

00:24:43.770 --> 00:24:45.600
I can hover over--
in this case, you

00:24:45.600 --> 00:24:50.430
can see the Spike Arrest policy
barely took even a millisecond.

00:24:50.430 --> 00:24:52.770
So many of these
policies have been

00:24:52.770 --> 00:24:58.680
very optimized to ensure
neural network level latency.

00:24:58.680 --> 00:25:01.680
Let's now try and
pretend that someone

00:25:01.680 --> 00:25:05.040
is trying to call the APIs
more than they should.

00:25:05.040 --> 00:25:08.720
And let's see what
the API platform does.

00:25:08.720 --> 00:25:11.540
So when I tried to
hit it a few times,

00:25:11.540 --> 00:25:14.390
right away the Spike
Arrest policy kicks in.

00:25:14.390 --> 00:25:16.830
It was set to three per minute.

00:25:16.830 --> 00:25:19.310
And in this case, it
calculates they're only

00:25:19.310 --> 00:25:24.170
going to allow three
transactions per minute thereby

00:25:24.170 --> 00:25:25.430
protecting your back-end APIs.

00:25:29.210 --> 00:25:30.100
Over to you, Greg.

00:25:30.100 --> 00:25:30.891
GREG BRAIL: Thanks.

00:25:30.891 --> 00:25:32.890
So as you can see--
just taking a break here

00:25:32.890 --> 00:25:35.420
from trying to be scripted--
what Prithpal has done

00:25:35.420 --> 00:25:38.200
is he's shown that we can
create a proxy for an API

00:25:38.200 --> 00:25:39.490
and we can add policies.

00:25:39.490 --> 00:25:41.350
Now, we don't have
unlimited time,

00:25:41.350 --> 00:25:43.390
so you might ask
questions like, can I

00:25:43.390 --> 00:25:45.070
customize the response
that comes back

00:25:45.070 --> 00:25:46.524
from this Spike Arrest policy?

00:25:46.524 --> 00:25:48.190
Can I have different
Spike Arrest limits

00:25:48.190 --> 00:25:50.050
for different types of APIs?

00:25:50.050 --> 00:25:53.200
Can I have TLS turned
on and required?

00:25:53.200 --> 00:25:56.319
Can I transform the API response
like we showed on the demo

00:25:56.319 --> 00:25:56.860
this morning?

00:25:56.860 --> 00:25:59.055
And the answer is, yes, you
can do all those things.

00:25:59.055 --> 00:26:01.180
This product has been around
for a couple of years.

00:26:01.180 --> 00:26:02.980
We have a lot of large
enterprise customers

00:26:02.980 --> 00:26:05.992
who drive a lot of sometimes
obscure requirements into it.

00:26:05.992 --> 00:26:07.450
For the purposes
of the demo, we're

00:26:07.450 --> 00:26:09.340
not going to show every
single thing we could possibly

00:26:09.340 --> 00:26:11.714
do with the product, but we're
trying to give you an idea

00:26:11.714 --> 00:26:12.400
here.

00:26:12.400 --> 00:26:15.100
But let's go back to
the slides for a sec.

00:26:15.100 --> 00:26:17.590
So basically what we've
done is we've shown, hey,

00:26:17.590 --> 00:26:20.530
using the Apigee Edge we can
now create a proxy for the API

00:26:20.530 --> 00:26:21.757
where we can insert policies.

00:26:21.757 --> 00:26:24.340
So now instead of going directly
to my back-end and then going

00:26:24.340 --> 00:26:25.240
to Apigee--

00:26:25.240 --> 00:26:27.430
and it's pretty easy to
insert some basic policies

00:26:27.430 --> 00:26:31.780
like a Spike Arrest to give
a basic level of quality

00:26:31.780 --> 00:26:33.490
of service to my API.

00:26:33.490 --> 00:26:36.292
But of course, one of the things
I would think is, gee, great.

00:26:36.292 --> 00:26:37.250
You got a Spike Arrest.

00:26:37.250 --> 00:26:38.740
Now that people
watching on YouTube

00:26:38.740 --> 00:26:42.430
can't really mess things up, but
my API still has no security.

00:26:42.430 --> 00:26:43.950
And that's no good.

00:26:43.950 --> 00:26:45.430
I've read about OAuth.

00:26:45.430 --> 00:26:49.000
I understand that OAuth is
one of the de facto standards

00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:50.530
for API authentication.

00:26:50.530 --> 00:26:53.410
How can I start to add some
of those things to my API

00:26:53.410 --> 00:26:56.170
without having to learn all
the complexities of the spec

00:26:56.170 --> 00:26:57.520
and programming all myself?

00:26:57.520 --> 00:26:59.603
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: Now,
that's a great point, Greg.

00:26:59.603 --> 00:27:01.900
In fact, many customers
that we talked to,

00:27:01.900 --> 00:27:05.970
OAuth 2.0 is a very popular
API security standard.

00:27:05.970 --> 00:27:09.010
So in this segment, we're going
to take a look at how exactly

00:27:09.010 --> 00:27:11.770
we now take this API
and actually protect

00:27:11.770 --> 00:27:14.500
it using an OAuth policy.

00:27:14.500 --> 00:27:18.150
Let's switch to
the demo, please?

00:27:18.150 --> 00:27:20.400
Let's carry this thing
a little bit forward.

00:27:20.400 --> 00:27:26.830
I'm going to create and add a
very simple OAuth 2.0 policy.

00:27:26.830 --> 00:27:28.850
When I add this,
you can see that I'm

00:27:28.850 --> 00:27:33.350
trying to use the
VarifyAccessToken method.

00:27:33.350 --> 00:27:37.370
And the VarifyAccessToken
method within this OAuth policy

00:27:37.370 --> 00:27:42.590
is actually going to ensure
that this API, whenever invoked,

00:27:42.590 --> 00:27:46.580
is presented with a
valid OAuth token.

00:27:46.580 --> 00:27:51.850
Just for kicks, I'll go
back to my Postman client

00:27:51.850 --> 00:27:53.590
and hit a send request.

00:27:53.590 --> 00:27:56.140
And right away you can
see that it points out

00:27:56.140 --> 00:27:58.690
I need a valid token.

00:27:58.690 --> 00:28:00.640
So how do we go about
getting this token?

00:28:00.640 --> 00:28:03.210
One of the key parts
of the platform

00:28:03.210 --> 00:28:05.970
is the notion of
an OAuth server.

00:28:05.970 --> 00:28:08.675
So the Apigee Edge actually
has a built in OAuth

00:28:08.675 --> 00:28:11.700
server which can
mint tokens, and we

00:28:11.700 --> 00:28:15.850
support a variety of different
kinds of OAuth flows.

00:28:15.850 --> 00:28:18.780
We have the standard two-legged
and three-legged OAuth flows

00:28:18.780 --> 00:28:19.740
out of the box.

00:28:19.740 --> 00:28:22.030
And because the platform
is customizable,

00:28:22.030 --> 00:28:24.410
you can actually support
some other flows as well.

00:28:24.410 --> 00:28:28.170
Whether Apigee is the platform
that does the OAuth dance

00:28:28.170 --> 00:28:32.160
itself, or it integrates-- which
we have in the past with many

00:28:32.160 --> 00:28:34.320
different identity and
access management systems--

00:28:34.320 --> 00:28:35.720
it can control that.

00:28:35.720 --> 00:28:40.020
Mint tokens, take some
other systems tokens,

00:28:40.020 --> 00:28:41.970
and enforce that,
and all that stuff.

00:28:41.970 --> 00:28:43.860
In this case, in order
to actually show you

00:28:43.860 --> 00:28:48.040
how this would work, I'm going
to introduce a new concept,

00:28:48.040 --> 00:28:50.210
and that's of an API product.

00:28:50.210 --> 00:28:51.760
So what's an API product?

00:28:51.760 --> 00:28:55.720
An API product is actually the
way you would want to expose

00:28:55.720 --> 00:28:58.860
your capabilities-- which
are nothing but APIs--

00:28:58.860 --> 00:29:01.360
in different shapes
and sizes to consumers.

00:29:01.360 --> 00:29:02.980
So you could think
about a strategy

00:29:02.980 --> 00:29:07.460
where I could take
three APIs and only give

00:29:07.460 --> 00:29:11.720
read only access through
what we call an API product.

00:29:11.720 --> 00:29:14.510
So an API product--

00:29:14.510 --> 00:29:19.180
and I'll build something
out here called Hotels Gold.

00:29:22.680 --> 00:29:26.650
And you can assign
some quotas out here.

00:29:26.650 --> 00:29:29.850
In this case, let's
say 100 per minute.

00:29:29.850 --> 00:29:33.250
And I'm going to pick the
API proxy that we just

00:29:33.250 --> 00:29:36.930
built, which is Hotels.

00:29:36.930 --> 00:29:39.000
In this API product
definition, I

00:29:39.000 --> 00:29:41.010
can add a bunch of
different API proxies.

00:29:41.010 --> 00:29:44.850
And I can also limit them
by perhaps all of these APIs

00:29:44.850 --> 00:29:46.720
only have get access.

00:29:46.720 --> 00:29:49.425
GREG BRAIL: So you can limit
what I can do with the API,

00:29:49.425 --> 00:29:51.217
as well as which
parts of the API I

00:29:51.217 --> 00:29:52.800
can call, but also
what I can with it.

00:29:52.800 --> 00:29:53.220
OK.

00:29:53.220 --> 00:29:54.303
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: Exactly.

00:29:54.303 --> 00:29:57.150
And one of the other things I
could do is the exact same API

00:29:57.150 --> 00:29:59.550
combination-- or
perhaps a separate one--

00:29:59.550 --> 00:30:02.380
I could perhaps make a
hotel's platinum product.

00:30:02.380 --> 00:30:03.200
In which case--

00:30:03.200 --> 00:30:03.760
GREG BRAIL: For free?

00:30:03.760 --> 00:30:04.320
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: Yes.

00:30:04.320 --> 00:30:04.800
GREG BRAIL: Right.

00:30:04.800 --> 00:30:07.383
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: In which case,
give them access to more APIs

00:30:07.383 --> 00:30:10.050
and perhaps let you do more
with those APIs itself,

00:30:10.050 --> 00:30:14.430
and perhaps up your bandwidth
to a 1,000 times a minute.

00:30:14.430 --> 00:30:17.170
So let's go ahead and
save this API product.

00:30:17.170 --> 00:30:17.670
Great.

00:30:17.670 --> 00:30:20.310
So this is there when we
talk about the API team,

00:30:20.310 --> 00:30:23.640
you also have collaboration
with not only the API developer

00:30:23.640 --> 00:30:25.740
but the API product manager.

00:30:25.740 --> 00:30:29.480
If you think about data as
business and APIs as products,

00:30:29.480 --> 00:30:30.810
that's exactly what we mean.

00:30:30.810 --> 00:30:33.870
Now you can bundle these
API and then offer them

00:30:33.870 --> 00:30:35.310
to the outside world.

00:30:35.310 --> 00:30:37.800
So great, I have
a product, but I

00:30:37.800 --> 00:30:42.370
need to be able to showcase
some level of security access.

00:30:42.370 --> 00:30:46.620
So in this case, I'm going to
introduce a concept of an app.

00:30:46.620 --> 00:30:50.070
Think about an app that has the
ability to actually generate

00:30:50.070 --> 00:30:51.360
some API keys.

00:30:51.360 --> 00:30:57.030
So in this case, I'm
going to very quickly call

00:30:57.030 --> 00:31:03.700
this the Hotels Gold app,
pick a developer here.

00:31:03.700 --> 00:31:10.540
And in this case, I'm going to
pick the Hotels Gold product

00:31:10.540 --> 00:31:11.950
and hit Save.

00:31:11.950 --> 00:31:15.190
So all what I've done
so far is saying, great,

00:31:15.190 --> 00:31:17.560
I have an API product
called Hotels.

00:31:17.560 --> 00:31:20.380
Now let me generate an
app called Hotels app.

00:31:20.380 --> 00:31:24.460
This is the app which has an
identifier key ID and a secret

00:31:24.460 --> 00:31:27.650
which you would share with
the app developer who's

00:31:27.650 --> 00:31:29.970
going to consume the API.

00:31:29.970 --> 00:31:31.990
This is where the
consumption angle comes in.

00:31:31.990 --> 00:31:34.960
So you can pretend
you have 10 partners.

00:31:34.960 --> 00:31:37.120
Each one of them may
have a separate app.

00:31:37.120 --> 00:31:38.510
Why would you do that?

00:31:38.510 --> 00:31:40.670
You would do that so you
can track them separately,

00:31:40.670 --> 00:31:43.469
you can report usage,
and as Greg mentioned,

00:31:43.469 --> 00:31:45.010
you can do some
pretty exotic things.

00:31:45.010 --> 00:31:47.860
You could have some partners
who are subscribed to the Gold

00:31:47.860 --> 00:31:51.550
product and have access to
lesser parts of the API, part

00:31:51.550 --> 00:31:52.580
of the lower quota.

00:31:52.580 --> 00:31:55.960
Or you can create apps
that give access to,

00:31:55.960 --> 00:31:58.270
let's say, the Hotel's
Platinum product,

00:31:58.270 --> 00:32:00.830
which give you more
access to the same APIs

00:32:00.830 --> 00:32:03.790
and additional stuff for
some of the other APIs.

00:32:03.790 --> 00:32:06.340
Once I have the app--

00:32:06.340 --> 00:32:08.060
let's click on this--

00:32:08.060 --> 00:32:12.430
I have what we call
as a key and secret.

00:32:12.430 --> 00:32:14.770
So what I'm going to
do at this point is--

00:32:14.770 --> 00:32:18.550
as I mentioned Apigee Edge also
has a full blown OAuth provider

00:32:18.550 --> 00:32:19.840
in the platform.

00:32:19.840 --> 00:32:23.800
I'm actually going to use a
pre-configured token endpoint,

00:32:23.800 --> 00:32:27.630
which is enabled by default in
any OAuth that you provision.

00:32:27.630 --> 00:32:33.130
And I'm going to specify
the client ID and the client

00:32:33.130 --> 00:32:34.976
secret.

00:32:34.976 --> 00:32:36.350
So what I'm really
doing out here

00:32:36.350 --> 00:32:40.160
is I'm mimicking that I am
an external client, which you

00:32:40.160 --> 00:32:41.720
have configured using an app.

00:32:41.720 --> 00:32:45.560
And someone has shared the
app key and secret with me.

00:32:45.560 --> 00:32:48.440
Traditionally, the sharing
of the app key and secret

00:32:48.440 --> 00:32:51.140
essentially happens
through a developer portal.

00:32:51.140 --> 00:32:53.520
That's on the consumption side.

00:32:53.520 --> 00:32:54.290
So I call this.

00:32:54.290 --> 00:32:56.060
And I'm using the
client credentials grant

00:32:56.060 --> 00:32:59.390
type, which may be very familiar
to some of the folks who are

00:32:59.390 --> 00:33:01.730
perhaps API developers already.

00:33:01.730 --> 00:33:06.710
When I make a request out here,
it gives me an access token.

00:33:06.710 --> 00:33:11.620
So I now have an access token
which can be used securely

00:33:11.620 --> 00:33:13.150
to access the API.

00:33:13.150 --> 00:33:15.700
This access token
could be timed.

00:33:15.700 --> 00:33:18.064
It can also be
scoped, where in it

00:33:18.064 --> 00:33:19.980
can give access for
different parts of the API

00:33:19.980 --> 00:33:21.170
within the product.

00:33:21.170 --> 00:33:23.560
At the product level
you can actually

00:33:23.560 --> 00:33:27.460
configure what kind of
scopes it allows access to.

00:33:27.460 --> 00:33:30.770
Let's take this access token.

00:33:30.770 --> 00:33:33.340
We'll go back to
the Hotels API proxy

00:33:33.340 --> 00:33:36.200
and now add an
authorization header here.

00:33:38.760 --> 00:33:43.470
And the exact same token
that was just minted,

00:33:43.470 --> 00:33:46.660
we'll place it in the
authorization header

00:33:46.660 --> 00:33:48.590
and now make a request--

00:33:48.590 --> 00:33:49.930
whoops.

00:33:49.930 --> 00:33:52.757
GREG BRAIL: Maybe we
didn't cut and paste right.

00:33:52.757 --> 00:33:53.840
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: Let me--

00:33:53.840 --> 00:33:55.270
oh.

00:33:55.270 --> 00:33:57.490
GREG BRAIL: That's your
client ID, I think.

00:33:57.490 --> 00:33:59.865
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: I think I
may have the wrong client ID.

00:33:59.865 --> 00:34:03.270
Let's go take a look
here and make sure.

00:34:03.270 --> 00:34:04.720
This is a live demo, guys.

00:34:04.720 --> 00:34:11.455
GREG BRAIL: [LAUGHS]
Yeah, that's right.

00:34:11.455 --> 00:34:12.330
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: OK.

00:34:12.330 --> 00:34:14.420
Let's see this and
get it out here.

00:34:22.409 --> 00:34:23.130
Oh.

00:34:23.130 --> 00:34:26.480
I know what the problem is,
and I know how to fix it.

00:34:26.480 --> 00:34:27.480
GREG BRAIL: It's a demo.

00:34:27.480 --> 00:34:29.406
Oh, we didn't set
up-- did we not?

00:34:29.406 --> 00:34:30.280
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: No.

00:34:30.280 --> 00:34:33.780
There's one thing which
we actually skipped over,

00:34:33.780 --> 00:34:35.790
and that was this
back-end system

00:34:35.790 --> 00:34:40.264
that I'm hitting actually has
its own security mechanism.

00:34:40.264 --> 00:34:40.889
GREG BRAIL: Oh.

00:34:40.889 --> 00:34:43.097
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: And this
is all writing the header,

00:34:43.097 --> 00:34:44.429
so let's show them--

00:34:44.429 --> 00:34:45.989
GREG BRAIL: Because we're
sending your token to GitHub.

00:34:45.989 --> 00:34:46.650
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: Exactly.

00:34:46.650 --> 00:34:48.358
Let's show them how
we can actually use--

00:34:48.358 --> 00:34:50.694
GREG BRAIL: Yeah, let's
take that access token out.

00:34:50.694 --> 00:34:52.610
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: And
let's remove that token.

00:34:52.610 --> 00:34:56.469
So here's another
key thing out here.

00:34:56.469 --> 00:34:58.630
As you're minting
these tokens, they

00:34:58.630 --> 00:35:00.310
may be back-end
systems, which happen

00:35:00.310 --> 00:35:04.210
to be in this case, which is
sensitive to an OAuth header.

00:35:04.210 --> 00:35:06.370
You might want to
remove or disable

00:35:06.370 --> 00:35:08.170
that OAuth header from here.

00:35:08.170 --> 00:35:11.230
So in this case,
let's go and let's

00:35:11.230 --> 00:35:12.622
remove the authorization header.

00:35:17.350 --> 00:35:21.910
This wasn't scripted by the
way, just for the record.

00:35:21.910 --> 00:35:22.840
That's a live demo.

00:35:25.520 --> 00:35:27.950
So let's go remove the
