00:44:20.420 --> 00:44:23.990
Now, once I've done this, I
probably want it to scale.

00:44:23.990 --> 00:44:27.080
And I have to assume that the
Apigee Edge product running

00:44:27.080 --> 00:44:28.490
as a service can auto scale.

00:44:28.490 --> 00:44:29.810
It runs on top of Google Cloud.

00:44:29.810 --> 00:44:31.880
It even runs on top
of Amazon Cloud.

00:44:31.880 --> 00:44:33.650
All those capabilities
allow us to take

00:44:33.650 --> 00:44:35.080
advantage of auto scaling.

00:44:35.080 --> 00:44:36.830
And some of the
interesting things you can

00:44:36.830 --> 00:44:38.697
do with Edge as a service--

00:44:38.697 --> 00:44:41.280
we've done a lot of work to make
sure that it not only scales,

00:44:41.280 --> 00:44:42.270
but it's very reliable.

00:44:42.270 --> 00:44:44.450
So for instance,
everything in the product

00:44:44.450 --> 00:44:46.940
is replicated
across data centers.

00:44:46.940 --> 00:44:48.530
We use technologies
like Cassandra.

00:44:48.530 --> 00:44:50.460
Remember, we run in a
lot of different places,

00:44:50.460 --> 00:44:52.640
not just the Google
Cloud, which allows

00:44:52.640 --> 00:44:54.380
us to have things
like OAuth tokens,

00:44:54.380 --> 00:44:56.710
and quotas, and
things like that work

00:44:56.710 --> 00:44:59.570
even in the failure of an
entire geographical region.

00:44:59.570 --> 00:45:01.400
And our customers
who run on-premises,

00:45:01.400 --> 00:45:03.620
they almost always run in
their own data centers.

00:45:03.620 --> 00:45:06.460
And they almost always have
more than one data center.

00:45:06.460 --> 00:45:09.290
And none of them has asked us--
actually one person asked us--

00:45:09.290 --> 00:45:12.740
for the old traditional
failover disaster recovery

00:45:12.740 --> 00:45:14.234
drill on the weekend thing.

00:45:14.234 --> 00:45:15.650
What they want is
everything to be

00:45:15.650 --> 00:45:18.560
active active across their
data centers at all time.

00:45:18.560 --> 00:45:20.870
Now, this chart here
shows the common case

00:45:20.870 --> 00:45:24.200
of auto scaling,
which is most APIs--

00:45:24.200 --> 00:45:26.030
just like most web
apps have a chart

00:45:26.030 --> 00:45:27.770
that looks kind of like this.

00:45:27.770 --> 00:45:29.600
You have big traffic
peaks Monday,

00:45:29.600 --> 00:45:31.640
Wednesday, Thursday,
and Friday, and whatever

00:45:31.640 --> 00:45:33.040
geography you're in.

00:45:33.040 --> 00:45:35.370
You have smaller traffic
peaks on weekends,

00:45:35.370 --> 00:45:36.730
and it's pretty predictable.

00:45:36.730 --> 00:45:39.350
And auto scaling systems, like
the ones built into the Cloud,

00:45:39.350 --> 00:45:40.370
can easily keep up.

00:45:40.370 --> 00:45:41.990
Because every 10
or 15 minutes they

00:45:41.990 --> 00:45:43.520
kind of average
what's happening,

00:45:43.520 --> 00:45:46.270
and they add servers
and take them away.

00:45:46.270 --> 00:45:48.890
And interesting use cases, we
have some customers whose peak

00:45:48.890 --> 00:45:50.070
does not look like this.

00:45:50.070 --> 00:45:52.490
So for instance, we have
a customer in the clothing

00:45:52.490 --> 00:45:56.270
industry that does auctions of
items of clothing periodically.

00:45:56.270 --> 00:45:58.650
So for instance, at
9:00 AM on Thursday,

00:45:58.650 --> 00:46:01.340
they'll auction a new limited
edition item of clothing

00:46:01.340 --> 00:46:03.980
to their fans who will use
their app to try and get

00:46:03.980 --> 00:46:05.270
this item of clothing.

00:46:05.270 --> 00:46:08.200
And their API traffic goes
from one or two requests

00:46:08.200 --> 00:46:10.730
a second, to 10,000
requests a second

00:46:10.730 --> 00:46:12.650
in the space of about a second.

00:46:12.650 --> 00:46:15.559
These kinds of auto
scaling algorithms

00:46:15.559 --> 00:46:17.600
don't work there, because
there's no auto scaling

00:46:17.600 --> 00:46:21.080
system in the world that can
detect traffic ramping up

00:46:21.080 --> 00:46:22.980
over a two or
three second period

00:46:22.980 --> 00:46:25.940
and spin up 50 or
100 server nodes.

00:46:25.940 --> 00:46:28.190
So we also have the ability
through our global support

00:46:28.190 --> 00:46:32.420
center to configure what the
parameters of this auto scaling

00:46:32.420 --> 00:46:33.190
are.

00:46:33.190 --> 00:46:36.740
And Apigee Edge-- we send cell
to a lot of large enterprises,

00:46:36.740 --> 00:46:39.800
and some of them like the idea
that they can call someone

00:46:39.800 --> 00:46:41.570
up and say, hey,
we have a big event

00:46:41.570 --> 00:46:42.710
happening tomorrow morning.

00:46:42.710 --> 00:46:44.240
Let's make sure we're ready.

00:46:44.240 --> 00:46:46.850
Let's kind of adjust those auto
scaling parameters in order

00:46:46.850 --> 00:46:48.500
to make this sort
of thing happen.

00:46:48.500 --> 00:46:51.150
That's a real important
thing that we do.

00:46:51.150 --> 00:46:53.130
Now, if we move on, of
course, none of this

00:46:53.130 --> 00:46:55.320
would work if we
weren't monitoring.

00:46:55.320 --> 00:46:56.642
So I talked about the scaling.

00:46:56.642 --> 00:46:58.350
I talked about all
those kinds of things,

00:46:58.350 --> 00:47:00.960
but obviously within our
global support centers

00:47:00.960 --> 00:47:02.470
we're monitoring
what's happening

00:47:02.470 --> 00:47:05.310
not only with the Cloud-- we
run on great Cloud platforms--

00:47:05.310 --> 00:47:07.710
but with our own application.

00:47:07.710 --> 00:47:10.140
Our certain API is using an
excessive amount of traffic,

00:47:10.140 --> 00:47:11.765
and we've gotten to
the point now where

00:47:11.765 --> 00:47:14.040
we have great people
working behind the scenes,

00:47:14.040 --> 00:47:16.260
as well as a lot of
automated monitoring

00:47:16.260 --> 00:47:19.290
where we often detect problems
with a customer's back-end API

00:47:19.290 --> 00:47:20.242
before they do.

00:47:20.242 --> 00:47:21.700
And sometimes we
call them and say,

00:47:21.700 --> 00:47:24.270
hey, we're noticing a lot
of errors from your API.

00:47:24.270 --> 00:47:27.600
We're noticing that the API
traffic is coming to Apigee,

00:47:27.600 --> 00:47:29.520
but it's not necessarily
getting to you.

00:47:29.520 --> 00:47:30.990
And they say, oh, look at that.

00:47:30.990 --> 00:47:32.460
We're having a problem.

00:47:32.460 --> 00:47:34.542
So it's a very
interesting world that we

00:47:34.542 --> 00:47:36.000
live in where we're
kind of sitting

00:47:36.000 --> 00:47:39.364
in the middle of a
lot of API traffic.

00:47:39.364 --> 00:47:41.780
But since we're sitting in the
middle of this API traffic,

00:47:41.780 --> 00:47:44.690
there's some interesting stuff
we can do about analyzing it.

00:47:44.690 --> 00:47:47.600
All the API calls
passing through Apigee

00:47:47.600 --> 00:47:48.740
we can gather metadata.

00:47:48.740 --> 00:47:52.400
Because we do things like
OAuth and API key validation,

00:47:52.400 --> 00:47:56.840
we can record for you which
developers, which applications,

00:47:56.840 --> 00:48:01.184
which geographical locations
are accessing the API the most,

00:48:01.184 --> 00:48:03.350
and allow you to do some
interesting things in order

00:48:03.350 --> 00:48:05.170
to run your API program.

00:48:05.170 --> 00:48:07.610
Remember, in a lot of cases
you may have an API product

00:48:07.610 --> 00:48:10.130
management team that is
actually trying to turn

00:48:10.130 --> 00:48:12.050
this API into a product.

00:48:12.050 --> 00:48:14.930
And even if it's not-- even
if it's just for customers,

00:48:14.930 --> 00:48:16.250
or partners--

00:48:16.250 --> 00:48:17.960
it's very important
that you understand,

00:48:17.960 --> 00:48:20.180
what are the most popular APIs?

00:48:20.180 --> 00:48:21.860
What kind of SLA am I achieving?

00:48:21.860 --> 00:48:23.480
Are my APIs very slow?

00:48:23.480 --> 00:48:26.030
Do they have a high error rate?

00:48:26.030 --> 00:48:29.390
Are certain applications
more popular than others?

00:48:29.390 --> 00:48:30.730
Are there giant changes?

00:48:30.730 --> 00:48:33.290
Has an application suddenly
gotten wildly popular?

00:48:33.290 --> 00:48:36.110
Which might mean either you
have a hit on your hands,

00:48:36.110 --> 00:48:39.290
or someone has compromised
some application credentials,

00:48:39.290 --> 00:48:41.840
and they're trying to use
the API in a way that's

00:48:41.840 --> 00:48:43.082
not supposed to be used.

00:48:43.082 --> 00:48:44.540
So for all these
reasons, there are

00:48:44.540 --> 00:48:47.180
a lot of analytical
capabilities in the product.

00:48:47.180 --> 00:48:49.400
And let's see if we can take
a look at how those work

00:48:49.400 --> 00:48:51.290
and what kind of
information you can get.

00:48:51.290 --> 00:48:52.248
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: Sure.

00:48:52.248 --> 00:48:54.800
Let's switch to
the demo, please?

00:48:54.800 --> 00:48:57.760
So back to the
Apigee Edge UI, you

00:48:57.760 --> 00:49:01.180
can click on the analyze
tab, and this gives you

00:49:01.180 --> 00:49:03.790
options around many
out of the box reports

00:49:03.790 --> 00:49:06.970
that are generated by
default by the platform.

00:49:06.970 --> 00:49:09.160
So I was talking
about you have an API

00:49:09.160 --> 00:49:12.340
proxy that's built
as API traffic is

00:49:12.340 --> 00:49:14.710
piping through the gateway.

00:49:14.710 --> 00:49:17.350
We collect a lot of
different kinds of metrics.

00:49:17.350 --> 00:49:19.490
Many of them we
kind of publish out

00:49:19.490 --> 00:49:21.400
of the box in terms
of these reports.

00:49:21.400 --> 00:49:24.550
So the very first one out here
is the API proxy performance

00:49:24.550 --> 00:49:25.640
report.

00:49:25.640 --> 00:49:29.080
Now, this gives you
a very broad idea

00:49:29.080 --> 00:49:31.570
of how the API
proxies are doing.

00:49:31.570 --> 00:49:34.630
And which means the
general amount API traffic

00:49:34.630 --> 00:49:36.140
piping through a system.

00:49:36.140 --> 00:49:38.730
In this case, I have a
few different API proxies

00:49:38.730 --> 00:49:40.630
which are deployed in this ORG.

00:49:40.630 --> 00:49:42.970
And you can see the
total traffic coming out

00:49:42.970 --> 00:49:44.140
through here.

00:49:44.140 --> 00:49:46.950
In a very quick snapshot--
the kind of number of errors,

00:49:46.950 --> 00:49:49.070
the percentage of error,
so on and so forth.

00:49:49.070 --> 00:49:50.990
You can also see
the amount of time

00:49:50.990 --> 00:49:54.910
which is being spent in the API
proxy versus the average target

00:49:54.910 --> 00:49:55.690
response time.

00:49:55.690 --> 00:49:56.500
GREG BRAIL: So
the amount of time

00:49:56.500 --> 00:49:58.390
that Apigee might
be taking to execute

00:49:58.390 --> 00:49:59.710
those policies I put in?

00:49:59.710 --> 00:50:00.100
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: Exactly.

00:50:00.100 --> 00:50:01.060
GREG BRAIL: Keeping
in mind, policies

00:50:01.060 --> 00:50:03.250
are sometimes simple
things like Spike Arrest,

00:50:03.250 --> 00:50:05.800
or sometimes they're my own
JavaScript code, or my own Java

00:50:05.800 --> 00:50:06.299
code.

00:50:06.299 --> 00:50:09.967
And that can be any number
of milliseconds, right?

00:50:09.967 --> 00:50:11.050
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: Exactly.

00:50:11.050 --> 00:50:12.610
So this starts to
give you a good idea

00:50:12.610 --> 00:50:14.740
of the kind of things you
should be doing within the API

00:50:14.740 --> 00:50:15.400
platform.

00:50:15.400 --> 00:50:16.900
Certainly, we've
run into situations

00:50:16.900 --> 00:50:19.780
where customers are doing some
heavy weight transformation.

00:50:19.780 --> 00:50:22.196
There are some other tools
which are really good for that.

00:50:22.196 --> 00:50:24.580
Your API platform is
the tier of agility.

00:50:24.580 --> 00:50:26.320
This is your face to
the outside world.

00:50:26.320 --> 00:50:28.420
You want to keep it as
quickly as possible,

00:50:28.420 --> 00:50:30.617
process as many
transactions as possible.

00:50:30.617 --> 00:50:32.200
There are a bunch
of different reports

00:50:32.200 --> 00:50:33.950
which we're not going
to go through today.

00:50:33.950 --> 00:50:35.820
But again, you
can check them out

00:50:35.820 --> 00:50:38.320
if you have a free trial
account, which anyone

00:50:38.320 --> 00:50:40.810
out here can sign up for today.

00:50:40.810 --> 00:50:42.910
But what I am going to
touch on very briefly

00:50:42.910 --> 00:50:45.790
is the notion of
customer reports.

00:50:45.790 --> 00:50:49.840
So although a lot of these out
of the box reports are great,

00:50:49.840 --> 00:50:52.150
but many times you
had the requirement

00:50:52.150 --> 00:50:56.590
if-- think about API has
become your digital channel.

00:50:56.590 --> 00:50:59.780
Businesses want to know what
kind of orders am I processing.

00:50:59.780 --> 00:51:00.280
Right?

00:51:00.280 --> 00:51:02.440
What kind of hotel reservations
have gone through this?

00:51:02.440 --> 00:51:04.273
So you need the ability
to be able to create

00:51:04.273 --> 00:51:07.480
custom, which can be
presented to a business user.

00:51:07.480 --> 00:51:10.090
In this case, I'll just
give a very simple example

00:51:10.090 --> 00:51:13.200
of a custom report.

00:51:13.200 --> 00:51:16.830
In this case, it just shows you
the amount of traffic coming in

00:51:16.830 --> 00:51:17.960
from different APIs.

00:51:17.960 --> 00:51:20.460
Now, these APIs could represent
different business segments,

00:51:20.460 --> 00:51:23.490
such as orders, such as
payments, so on and so forth.

00:51:23.490 --> 00:51:26.160
And using some
simple heuristics,

00:51:26.160 --> 00:51:28.170
we aggregate the data.

00:51:28.170 --> 00:51:30.720
And then you have the ability
to kind of dissect through this

00:51:30.720 --> 00:51:33.930
and figure out what's really
going on with the APIs.

00:51:33.930 --> 00:51:35.400
You can delve deeper.

00:51:35.400 --> 00:51:38.940
We have a bunch of different
kinds of metrics out here.

00:51:38.940 --> 00:51:41.580
If you were to click
in the edit tab here,

00:51:41.580 --> 00:51:45.530
you can just add different
out of the box metrics, which

00:51:45.530 --> 00:51:48.270
are available at a high level--

00:51:48.270 --> 00:51:50.505
all that traditional stuff
that you can expect to be.

00:51:50.505 --> 00:51:53.770
You can also see some
custom dimensions out here.

00:51:53.770 --> 00:51:57.180
These are the ones which
you as an API developer

00:51:57.180 --> 00:52:00.370
can have the ability to
create some custom dimensions.

00:52:00.370 --> 00:52:03.690
So imagine recapture
usually any information that

00:52:03.690 --> 00:52:06.630
can be gleaned on from
the header information,

00:52:06.630 --> 00:52:09.720
or any kind of commonly
available user agent

00:52:09.720 --> 00:52:10.950
stuff that's available.

00:52:10.950 --> 00:52:13.770
But as an API developer,
within the API proxy

00:52:13.770 --> 00:52:16.620
you can actually add policies,
such as a statistics collector

00:52:16.620 --> 00:52:19.590
policy, and that will
start scraping out

00:52:19.590 --> 00:52:21.030
all the useful
analytics that you

00:52:21.030 --> 00:52:25.620
need-- in this case, the order
amount and the order discount.

00:52:25.620 --> 00:52:28.629
Once you do that, you can add
various kinds of dimensions.

00:52:28.629 --> 00:52:31.170
And you can see the amount of
stuff that's available to you--

00:52:31.170 --> 00:52:34.770
some common dimensions which
you can slice and dice using

00:52:34.770 --> 00:52:37.750
the geographic region
that you're coming from,

00:52:37.750 --> 00:52:41.580
any specific developer app
perhaps who's calling it.

00:52:41.580 --> 00:52:43.830
You can use some custom
dimensions out here,

00:52:43.830 --> 00:52:45.450
and many of the
advanced dimensions

00:52:45.450 --> 00:52:47.970
are available for you
to use by default.

00:52:47.970 --> 00:52:51.540
Once you do this, you actually
have a very simple report,

00:52:51.540 --> 00:52:53.760
which can then be
shared in a secure way

00:52:53.760 --> 00:52:55.379
by different kinds of users.

00:52:55.379 --> 00:52:57.420
GREG BRAIL: And of course,
the reports have APIs.

00:52:57.420 --> 00:52:58.503
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: Exactly.

00:52:58.503 --> 00:53:00.440
GREG BRAIL: So you can
query them from an API.

00:53:00.440 --> 00:53:00.680
Cool.

00:53:00.680 --> 00:53:01.110
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: OK.

00:53:01.110 --> 00:53:01.624
Back to you.

00:53:01.624 --> 00:53:03.540
GREG BRAIL: So back to
the slides for just one

00:53:03.540 --> 00:53:05.130
more second.

00:53:05.130 --> 00:53:06.570
So that's how the
analytics works.

00:53:06.570 --> 00:53:08.695
So we've got some really
comprehensive capabilities

00:53:08.695 --> 00:53:09.390
there.

00:53:09.390 --> 00:53:11.848
And the last thing is, let's
talk a little bit about money.

00:53:11.848 --> 00:53:13.350
I want to make some
money on my API.

00:53:13.350 --> 00:53:14.670
Now, it turns out
there are a lot of ways

00:53:14.670 --> 00:53:15.870
to make money on your API.

00:53:15.870 --> 00:53:18.510
Back on our Hotels
API, I might make money

00:53:18.510 --> 00:53:20.640
by having people
stay in my hotels.

00:53:20.640 --> 00:53:22.740
Or sometimes I make
money because my API

00:53:22.740 --> 00:53:24.330
drives other product sales.

00:53:24.330 --> 00:53:26.680
But I might be doing
something really interesting.

00:53:26.680 --> 00:53:29.550
I might be in finance, and I'm
trying to do a market data API.

00:53:29.550 --> 00:53:31.320
Those folks are
actually next door.

00:53:31.320 --> 00:53:33.090
Or I might be in the
news, and I might

00:53:33.090 --> 00:53:35.220
charge a lot of money
for extremely, extremely

00:53:35.220 --> 00:53:37.710
timely news, or who knows what.

00:53:37.710 --> 00:53:40.560
I might want to actually
charge per API call

00:53:40.560 --> 00:53:42.750
and have different tiers,
and different kinds

00:53:42.750 --> 00:53:44.760
of prices for
different API calls.

00:53:44.760 --> 00:53:46.552
Is there a way that
I could do that?

00:53:46.552 --> 00:53:47.760
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: Absolutely.

00:53:47.760 --> 00:53:50.760
Let's switch to the
demo again, please?

00:53:50.760 --> 00:53:53.460
This is actually by design,
going from slides to demo, just

00:53:53.460 --> 00:53:54.669
to make sure you guys are up.

00:53:54.669 --> 00:53:56.876
GREG BRAIL: The person taking
the screen apart is not

00:53:56.876 --> 00:53:58.345
by design, but that's okay.

00:53:58.345 --> 00:53:59.642
[LAUGHTER]

00:53:59.642 --> 00:54:02.100
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: So let's use
a live example in this case.

00:54:02.100 --> 00:54:04.740
I'm going to very quickly talk
about Pitney Bowes, which was

00:54:04.740 --> 00:54:06.850
also mentioned in the Keynote.

00:54:06.850 --> 00:54:08.940
And this happens through
a developer portal.

00:54:08.940 --> 00:54:11.280
So I give you a very
broad and very high

00:54:11.280 --> 00:54:13.770
level example of how
you could take and build

00:54:13.770 --> 00:54:14.786
a developer portal.

00:54:14.786 --> 00:54:17.160
This is another example of
how they take in the developer

00:54:17.160 --> 00:54:18.870
portal and customize it.

00:54:18.870 --> 00:54:21.330
And they also happen
to leverage some

00:54:21.330 --> 00:54:24.330
of the monetization capabilities
of the Apigee Edge platform.

00:54:24.330 --> 00:54:26.850
If you think about
monetization, once you

00:54:26.850 --> 00:54:28.890
hit the level of
maturity in the APIs,

00:54:28.890 --> 00:54:31.070
and you want to make
money off your APIs--

00:54:31.070 --> 00:54:33.270
which would seem
natural at some point--

00:54:33.270 --> 00:54:36.240
you can use some of the
monetization capabilities

00:54:36.240 --> 00:54:38.250
provided by Apigee Edge.

00:54:38.250 --> 00:54:40.210
We're going to review
the consumer side of it,

00:54:40.210 --> 00:54:41.820
but from the producers side--

00:54:41.820 --> 00:54:43.500
or the providers side of APIs--

00:54:43.500 --> 00:54:45.570
you can create
different grid plans.

00:54:45.570 --> 00:54:47.640
Because you may
want to offer APIs

00:54:47.640 --> 00:54:50.280
so that people can consume
based on different kinds

00:54:50.280 --> 00:54:51.550
of dimensions.

00:54:51.550 --> 00:54:54.560
Perhaps you want to sell APIs
using volume based pricing.

00:54:54.560 --> 00:54:55.210
Right?

00:54:55.210 --> 00:54:57.480
Maybe these need a number
of API calls per month,

00:54:57.480 --> 00:54:59.370
or you may want to
charge separately

00:54:59.370 --> 00:55:00.246
for each transaction.

00:55:00.246 --> 00:55:02.620
GREG BRAIL: Or you might want
to do revenue sharing where

00:55:02.620 --> 00:55:04.560
you give developers
money for building apps

00:55:04.560 --> 00:55:05.430
that use your API.

00:55:05.430 --> 00:55:06.513
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: Exactly.

00:55:06.513 --> 00:55:09.080
So Walgreens is another example
of how they use revenue share

00:55:09.080 --> 00:55:11.070
when they have
their partners who

00:55:11.070 --> 00:55:12.720
are sending business to them.

00:55:12.720 --> 00:55:15.990
So the developers get a cut
of that transaction amount.

00:55:15.990 --> 00:55:18.240
So in this Pitney
Bowes developer portal,

00:55:18.240 --> 00:55:20.880
you can see they have
different kinds of APIs.

00:55:20.880 --> 00:55:25.090
I'm going to click
on the GeoSearch API

00:55:25.090 --> 00:55:26.830
and click on pricing plans.

00:55:26.830 --> 00:55:29.490
This gives you the
consumer side of it.

00:55:29.490 --> 00:55:31.690
So if you are an app
developer trying to use

00:55:31.690 --> 00:55:34.070
some of the Pitney Bowes API--

00:55:34.070 --> 00:55:36.220
and this gives GeoSearch--
you can sign up

00:55:36.220 --> 00:55:38.050
for different kinds of plans.

00:55:38.050 --> 00:55:40.810
In this case, they
are doing volume

00:55:40.810 --> 00:55:45.610
based pricing where you
can get 5k, 10k, et cetera,

00:55:45.610 --> 00:55:46.900
and different credits.

00:55:46.900 --> 00:55:49.240
In this case, you can
build custom plans.

00:55:49.240 --> 00:55:52.110
So this gives you one glimpse
of how you can use monetization

00:55:52.110 --> 00:55:54.110
both from the provider's
side, and in this case,

00:55:54.110 --> 00:55:56.499
from the consumer side,
to now monetize you APIs.

00:55:56.499 --> 00:55:57.790
GREG BRAIL: So what's a credit?

00:55:57.790 --> 00:56:00.907
Is that like different points
for different API calls?

00:56:00.907 --> 00:56:01.990
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: Exactly.

00:56:01.990 --> 00:56:03.573
So in this case,
they have this notion

00:56:03.573 --> 00:56:07.240
of some kind of a
freemium tier where

00:56:07.240 --> 00:56:08.740
when you're using
a higher tier they

00:56:08.740 --> 00:56:10.180
give you more and
more credits, so

00:56:10.180 --> 00:56:11.590
the upside for the developers--

00:56:11.590 --> 00:56:12.200
GREG BRAIL: Oh, I see.

00:56:12.200 --> 00:56:12.430
That's interesting.

00:56:12.430 --> 00:56:13.570
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: --to
use more and more APIs.

00:56:13.570 --> 00:56:14.170
Exactly.

00:56:14.170 --> 00:56:15.400
GREG BRAIL: And
different API calls

00:56:15.400 --> 00:56:16.975
could have different
weights, essentially.

00:56:16.975 --> 00:56:17.350
Right?

00:56:17.350 --> 00:56:17.920
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: Exactly.

00:56:17.920 --> 00:56:18.190
GREG BRAIL: OK.

00:56:18.190 --> 00:56:18.890
Cool.

00:56:18.890 --> 00:56:19.240
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: Awesome.

00:56:19.240 --> 00:56:19.865
So back to you.

00:56:19.865 --> 00:56:20.490
GREG BRAIL: OK.

00:56:20.490 --> 00:56:22.145
So back to the
slides one last time.

00:56:24.904 --> 00:56:26.320
So what we've done
really is we've

00:56:26.320 --> 00:56:28.155
gone through the whole
lifecycle of APIs.

00:56:28.155 --> 00:56:30.280
We've talked about everything
from designing a spec

00:56:30.280 --> 00:56:32.620
and publishing the
spec as documentation,

00:56:32.620 --> 00:56:35.080
to creating an API proxy
and adding policies.

00:56:35.080 --> 00:56:36.910
So I can have some of
the different levels

00:56:36.910 --> 00:56:40.870
of service around my API without
changing what's on my back-end.

00:56:40.870 --> 00:56:42.970
We talked about how to
create a developer portal

00:56:42.970 --> 00:56:46.210
and actually publish that
API for use by developers.

00:56:46.210 --> 00:56:49.750
How I can use analytics to find
out how my API is being used.

00:56:49.750 --> 00:56:51.640
And if I want to
charge for my API--

00:56:51.640 --> 00:56:54.970
like Pitney Bowes is doing
using the Apigee Edge product--

00:56:54.970 --> 00:56:56.890
how we can do that as well.

00:56:56.890 --> 00:56:58.960
So basically that's what
we do with Apigee Edge.

00:56:58.960 --> 00:57:00.640
